WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.515
Book Four, Proposition Nine.

2
00:00:02.515 --> 00:00:05.957
About a given square to circumscribe a circle.

3
00:00:05.957 --> 00:00:13.290
Let A B C D be the given square; it is required to circumscribe a circle about the square A B C D.

4
00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:19.760
Join the diagonals A C and B D, and let them cut one another at E.

5
00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:32.484
Because D A equals A B, A C is common, and base D C equals base B C, by One Eight angle D A C equals angle B A C, so angle D A B is bisected by A C.

6
00:00:32.484 --> 00:00:40.382
Similarly, each of the angles A B C, B C D, and C D A is bisected by the diagonals A C and B D.

7
00:00:40.382 --> 00:00:51.107
Since angle D A B equals angle A B C, their halves—angles E A B and E B A—are also equal, so by One Six side E A equals side E B.

8
00:00:51.107 --> 00:00:58.506
By the same reasoning, all four segments E A, E B, E C, and E D are equal to one another.

9
00:00:58.506 --> 00:01:12.140
Therefore the circle E A, described with center E and defining radius point A, passes through B, C, and D, circumscribing the square A B C D, as was required to do.

10
00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:19.702
Proposition Four Nine completes Euclid's four-part duality between the square and the circle across Propositions Six through Nine.

11
00:01:19.702 --> 00:01:37.064
As Heath observes, while any triangle admits both an inscribed and a circumscribed circle, a general quadrilateral does not: only through the symmetry of the square do the diagonals bisect the right-angled corners into forty-five-degree isosceles triangles whose common vertex E is equidistant from all four corners.

12
00:01:37.064 --> 00:01:50.360
In the GEOMETOR G-Index, node G one hundred sixty-one synthesizes ten direct prerequisites across thirty-two ancestor nodes, uniting Side-Side-Side congruence and the converse isosceles theorem with the definitions of square and circumcircle.

13
00:01:50.780 --> 00:02:05.660
